Stop the permission prompt from re-anchoring itself to the same place.

The geolocation prompt could not be answered on device. Every button --
"allow while visiting", "allow this time", "never allow" and the close X --
did nothing, and the page's getCurrentPosition() sat there until it failed
with TIMEOUT rather than PERMISSION_DENIED.

The input path was not at fault. Device traces showed the press reaching the
bubble's aura window with the right local coordinates and no capture held, the
gesture recognizer producing the full BEGIN/TAP_DOWN/SHOW_PRESS/TAP/END
sequence against that window, the Views hit test resolving the point to the
MdTextButton, and the button's own state going NORMAL -> PRESSED -> HOVERED.
That last step only happens inside views::Button::OnGestureEvent, so the
button's callback did run.

The callback is PermissionPromptBaseView::FilterUnintenedEventsAndRunCallbacks,
whose first act is a clickjacking check. It was rejecting every press:

  OHOS inputprot since_protect_ms=96 cooldown_ms=500 too_soon=1

The bubble had been on screen for seconds, yet its protection clock had been
restarted 96ms earlier -- between the press and the release of the very tap
being judged. Four restarts arrive together on each press, from two anchor
changes feeding two protectors:

  OHOS browserview layout bounds=0,0 377x810
  OHOS anchorchg rect=0,0 0x0
  OHOS anchorchg rect=40,0 0x0

BrowserView::Layout() calls PermissionRequestManager::UpdateAnchor(), which
lands in AnchorToPageInfoOrChip(). With no Views toolbar to anchor to -- this
port draws its browser UI in ArkTS -- the anchor configuration is null, so that
function clears the anchor and then sets a rect. BubbleDialogDelegate has no
"did it actually move" guard on either step: SetAnchorRect() notifies
unconditionally, so a stationary anchor still reports two moves, and each one
restarts the cooldown via UpdateInputProtectorsTimeStamp().

Upstream platforms never see this because they have a real location-bar anchor
view; SetAnchorView() with the same view returns early and nothing is reported.
The deadlock needs both halves -- a null anchor and a layout while the finger
is down -- and this port has both.

The fix makes the no-op case a no-op: if the prompt is already anchored to
nothing and the page-info rect it would compute is the rect it already has,
leave the anchor alone.

Verified on device (Huawei, HarmonyOS 7.0, 1316x2832): tapping "allow while
visiting" now closes the bubble, navigator.permissions reports granted, and
getCurrentPosition() returns a fix (39.88053, 116.59810, accuracy 23.5m) in
8.2s.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/views/permissions/permission_prompt_base_view.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/views/permissions/permission_prompt_base_view.cc
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/views/permissions/permission_prompt_base_view.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/views/permissions/permission_prompt_base_view.cc
@@ -135,6 +135,27 @@ void PermissionPromptBaseView::AnchorToPageInfoOrChip() {
   bubble_anchor_util::AnchorConfiguration configuration =
       bubble_anchor_util::GetPermissionPromptBubbleAnchorConfiguration(
           web_contents());
+
+  // Re-anchoring the bubble to where it already is has to be a no-op.
+  //
+  // When there is no toolbar to anchor to -- the case on HarmonyOS, where the
+  // browser UI is not built out of Views -- the code below clears the anchor
+  // and then sets a rect. BubbleDialogDelegate reports each of those as the
+  // anchor having moved, and every report restarts this prompt's clickjacking
+  // cooldown through UpdateInputProtectorsTimeStamp(). BrowserView::Layout()
+  // reaches here via PermissionRequestManager::UpdateAnchor(), so a layout
+  // that runs between a button's press and its release restarts the cooldown
+  // inside the ~100ms gap between the two, and
+  // FilterUnintenedEventsAndRunCallbacks() then discards the resulting tap as
+  // unintended. The prompt cannot be answered at all: not accepted, not
+  // denied, not dismissed.
+  if (configuration.anchor.IsNull() && GetAnchor().IsNull() &&
+      bubble_anchor_util::GetPageInfoAnchorRect(GetBrowser()) ==
+          GetAnchorRect()) {
+    SetArrow(configuration.bubble_arrow);
+    return;
+  }
+
   SetAnchor(configuration.anchor);
   // In fullscreen, `anchor` may be nullptr because the toolbar is hidden,
   // therefore anchor to the browser window instead.
